    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d267">

                <pb facs="0538" n="404" xml:id="pg404"/>

                <head>No. 267.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Shu Cheon
                    Pon.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-09-26"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 26,
                        1888</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I beg leave to acknowledge your note, dated
                    yesterday, in which you inform me that although the treaty signed and concluded
                    at this capital on the 12th of March last, between the United States and China,
                    seems to meet the views of your Government as to its general purpose, yet it
                    causes dissatisfaction to the Chinese people, and that there are three certain
                    points in the treaty which the Chinese Government regards it necessary should be
                    reconsidered and which by the instruction of your minister you set forth in your
                    note and bring to my notice.</p>
                <p>I am much pleased to learn by your note that the minister expected to sail from
                    Peru to-day for this country, and that we may expect his return to this capital
                    in the course of three weeks. His presence here will be assuredly welcomed, and
                    I trust that he will renew the duties of his high office in good health, and
                    continue in those relations to this Department which have always been so cordial
                    and harmonious.</p>
                <p>Accept, sir, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
